{{fbdoc item="title" value="curl"}}----
Free and easy-to-use client-side URL transfer library supporting almost every protocol.

Website: https://curl.se/libcurl/
Platforms supported: Windows (32-bit and 64-bit), Linux, macOS, FreeBSD, Haiku
Headers to include: curl.bi
Header version: 7.85.0
Examples: in examples/network/curl/

Haiku R1 beta 6 amd64: `examples/manual/libraries/curl.bas` compiled and ran
with FreeBASIC 1.20.4-3 against HaikuPorts libcurl 8.22.0, fetching and
checking the exact response from a local loopback HTTP fixture.

The declarations describe libcurl 7.85.0. The Windows qualification uses the installed 32-bit and 64-bit libcurl 8.21.0 SDKs and compares stable C types and form-list layouts with the native C headers. This does not add newer APIs to ##curl.bi##. The HTTP fixtures test loopback transfers; they do not establish HTTPS certificate or public-server interoperability.

The example accepts a URL as its first command-line argument. It prints the exact callback bytes, including embedded zeros, without adding a newline for each callback. It checks option and transfer errors, returns a failing process status for HTTP and transport errors, and balances global initialization and easy-handle cleanup. Its connection deadline is five seconds and its complete transfer deadline is fifteen seconds. ##CURLOPT_*## options which take C ##long## must receive a ##Clong## argument in varargs on 64-bit Windows.

The ##CHttp## examples build a static library from ##CHttp.bas##, ##CHttpStream.bas## and ##CHttpForm.bas## before linking ##test.bas##. Each connection owns its easy handle and header list. A stream with a supplied connection borrows it, so that connection must outlive the stream. These owning example objects are used synchronously on one thread and must not be copied.

##CHttpStream.read(TRUE)## returns exactly the response body, without the internal C terminator; a text read stops at the first zero. ##send()## uploads the caller's bytes synchronously using ##CURLOPT_UPLOAD## (PUT for HTTP), including an empty body. It keeps response storage separate from the borrowed upload buffer and supplies a seek callback so a redirected upload can be rewound. It removes stack-backed read and seek userdata before returning. ##read()## can retrieve the upload response. Transfer failures clear partial response data. The wrapper treats HTTP status policy as a caller option; the standalone manual example enables ##CURLOPT_FAILONERROR##.

##CHttpForm## retains the original ##curl_formadd## multipart interface, which libcurl has deprecated in favor of the MIME API. It remains an example of that original binding, and should be linked against a libcurl build which still exposes it.

See the libcurl [[https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION.html|write callback]], [[https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_UPLOAD.html|upload]], [[https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_SEEKFUNCTION.html|seek callback]], and [[https://curl.se/libcurl/c/curl_global_init.html|global initialization]] references for the underlying API contracts.

{{fbdoc item="ex"}}
{{fbdoc item="filename" value="examples/manual/libraries/curl.bas"}}%%(freebasic)
'' FreeBASIC manual examples
'' File: curl.bas
'' Purpose: Fetch a URL with libcurl's easy interface.
'' Responsibilities: Preserve callback bytes, report errors, and release curl state.
'' This file contains no redirects, retries or asynchronous transfers.
'' See Also: https://www.freebasic.net/wiki/wikka.php?wakka=ExtLibcurl

#include once "curl.bi"
#include once "crt/string.bi"

'' libcurl supplies a byte count, not a terminated string. A FreeBASIC STRING
'' preserves embedded zeros; the semicolon avoids adding callback-dependent
'' newlines. The temporary chunk belongs to this synchronous callback only.
private function write_callback cdecl ( byval buffer as byte ptr, _
                                        byval size as size_t, _
                                        byval nitems as size_t, _
                                        byval userdata as any ptr ) as size_t
	dim maximum as size_t = cast( size_t, -1 )
	if( size <> 0 andalso nitems > maximum \ size ) then return 0
	dim bytes as size_t = size * nitems
	if( bytes > (maximum shr 1) ) then return 0
	if( bytes <> 0 andalso buffer = NULL ) then return 0
	dim chunk as string = space( bytes )
	if( len( chunk ) <> bytes ) then return 0
	if( bytes <> 0 ) then memcpy( strptr( chunk ), buffer, bytes )
	print chunk;
	return bytes
end function

function main() as integer
	dim url as string = command(1)
	if( len( url ) = 0 ) then url = "https://freebasic.net/"
	if( curl_global_init( CURL_GLOBAL_ALL ) <> CURLE_OK ) then return 1
	dim handle as CURL ptr = curl_easy_init( )
	if( handle = NULL ) then
		curl_global_cleanup( )
		return 1
	end if

	'' Long options use C long in varargs, including on 64-bit Windows.
	'' Bound the wait to five seconds for connection and fifteen in total.
	dim as clong connect_timeout = 5000, total_timeout = 15000, fail_http = 1
	dim result as CURLcode = CURLE_FAILED_INIT
	do
		if( curl_easy_setopt( handle, CURLOPT_URL, strptr( url ) ) <> CURLE_OK ) then exit do
		if( curl_easy_setopt( handle, CURLOPT_WRITEFUNCTION, @write_callback ) <> CURLE_OK ) then exit do
		if( curl_easy_setopt( handle, CURLOPT_CONNECTTIMEOUT_MS, connect_timeout ) <> CURLE_OK ) then exit do
		if( curl_easy_setopt( handle, CURLOPT_TIMEOUT_MS, total_timeout ) <> CURLE_OK ) then exit do
		if( curl_easy_setopt( handle, CURLOPT_FAILONERROR, fail_http ) <> CURLE_OK ) then exit do
		result = curl_easy_perform( handle )
	loop while FALSE

	if( result <> CURLE_OK ) then print "curl transfer failed: "; *curl_easy_strerror( result )
	curl_easy_cleanup( handle )
	curl_global_cleanup( )
	if( result <> CURLE_OK ) then return 1
	return 0
end function

end main()

'' end of curl.bas
%%

